Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY'S COUNTING HOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE

Period live-action in Victorian London. Frost rims the windows. EBENEZER SCROOGE, a gaunt elderly man with a pointed nose, red-rimmed eyes, wispy white hair and wiry chin whiskers, hunches over a ledger in a severe black frock coat.

(SOUND: scratching quills, stamping feet outside, a distant church bell)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Marley was dead: to begin with. Old Marley was as dead as a door-nail. Yet his name remained above the warehouse door: Scrooge and Marley.

BOB CRATCHIT, a slight, brown-haired clerk in threadbare clothes and a long white comforter, warms his fingers over one fading coal.

FRED, Scrooge's ruddy, handsome young nephew in a brown greatcoat, strides in glowing from the cold.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure.

SCROOGE
What right have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? You're rich enough.

Bob smothers a smile behind his ledger.

FRED
I have always thought of Christmas as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time, when men and women open their shut-up hearts freely. Though it has never put gold or silver in my pocket, I believe it has done me good. God bless it!

Bob applauds once, then accidentally extinguishes his coal.

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation.

FRED
Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
I'll see you in that extremity first. Good afternoon.

Fred departs without an angry word. MR. DOBSON, a portly gentleman with grey whiskers, a brown coat and charitable subscription papers, enters and removes his hat.

MR. DOBSON
At this festive season, it is desirable that we make some provision for the Poor and destitute. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons? And the Union workhouses?

MR. DOBSON
Many can't go there, and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

MR. DOBSON
But you might know their suffering.

SCROOGE
It's not my business. Good afternoon, sir.

Mr. Dobson withdraws. Night gathers while Scrooge locks the ledger and Bob reaches for his hat.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient. A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December! Be here all the earlier next morning.

BOB CRATCHIT
Yes, sir. Merry Christmas, sir.

Scrooge answers with a growl.

EXT. SCROOGE'S DOORSTEP - NIGHT

Fog smothers the black old gateway. Scrooge, buttoned to his chin, puts his key into the lock beneath a massive brass knocker.

(SOUND: winter wind moaning through the courtyard)

The knocker becomes MARLEY'S livid face, its spectacles raised upon its forehead and its dead eyes fixed upon Scrooge.

(SOUND: a faint iron chain dragging behind the door)

Scrooge recoils. The face becomes a knocker again.

SCROOGE
Pooh, pooh!

He enters and slams the door.

(SOUND: the impact rolls through the empty house like thunder)

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

Scrooge, now in a faded dressing gown, slippers and nightcap, bends over a mean fire and a bowl of gruel. The cavernous chamber is double-locked.

(SOUND: one disused bell begins ringing, joined by every bell in the house)

The bells stop together. Scrooge grips his chair.

(SOUND: a heavy chain clanks in the cellar, mounts the stairs and crosses the landing)

MARLEY passes through the locked door: a transparent elderly ghost in pigtail, waistcoat, tights, boots and a burial cloth around his jaw. A chain of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers and steel purses coils around him.

SCROOGE
What do you want with me?

MARLEY
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese. There's more of gravy than of grave about you!

Marley tears away his burial cloth. His jaw drops upon his breast.

(SOUND: Marley shrieks and shakes his chain with a deafening iron crash)

Scrooge falls to his knees.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Why do you trouble me?

MARLEY
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard. Is its pattern strange to you?

SCROOGE
Speak comfort to me, Jacob!

MARLEY
I have none to give. Mankind was my business. Charity, mercy, forbearance and benevolence were all my business. The dealings of my trade were but a drop of water in the comprehensive ocean of my business!

SCROOGE
What must I do?

MARLEY
You will be haunted by Three Spirits. Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread.

The window rises by itself. Beyond it, chained phantoms reach helplessly toward the poor in the street.

(SOUND: spectral lamentations mingle with the bitter wind)

MARLEY
For your own sake, remember what has passed between us.

Marley floats into the night and vanishes among the phantoms. Scrooge stumbles to bed fully dressed and falls instantly asleep.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

Hours later, Scrooge lies awake beneath his bed-curtains, his gaunt face rigid with expectation.

(SOUND: the church clock tolls one deep, hollow stroke)

A clear light floods the bed. The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST draws the curtains: a child-sized, ageless figure with smooth young features, long white hair, a white flower-trimmed tunic, a green holly branch and a jet of light rising from its head.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past.

The Spirit offers its strong hand.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your reclamation. Rise, and walk with me.

Scrooge takes the hand. The walls dissolve into brilliant winter light.

(SOUND: a crystalline rush swallows the ticking clock)

INT. BOARDING SCHOOL CLASSROOM - WINTER DAY

A long, bare classroom stands deserted. BOY SCROOGE, a thin, pale child with untidy dark hair and solemn brown eyes, reads beside a feeble fire in a worn school coat.

(SOUND: mice scuffle behind the panels, water drips in the frozen yard)

The elderly Scrooge and the Ghost stand unseen beside him.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left here still.

Scrooge sits on a bench and weeps at the sight of himself.

SCROOGE
Poor boy. There was a boy singing a Christmas carol at my door last night. I should like to have given him something.

FAN, a delicate little girl with dark curls and a blue travelling cloak, bursts through the door.

(SOUND: carriage bells jingling outside)

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, for good and all. Father is so much kinder than he used to be. We are to be together all the Christmas long!

BOY SCROOGE
Home, little Fan?

She throws her arms around him. The children hurry away hand in hand.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
She had one child.

SCROOGE
One child.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your nephew.

Scrooge lowers his eyes.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE

YOUNG SCROOGE, now a lean apprentice with neat dark hair, keen brown eyes and a plain black coat, works beside another lad. Jolly FEZZIWIG, broad-bellied in a Welsh wig and bright waistcoat, springs from his high desk.

(MUSIC: a lively fiddle tuning into a country dance)

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, my boys! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve! Clear away, and let's have lots of room here!

Shutters fly into place. Desks vanish against the walls. Lamps blaze, food arrives and couples whirl across the floor while Fezziwig dances with his smiling wife.

(SOUND: stamping heels, clapping hands and abundant laughter)

The elderly Scrooge dances invisibly among the guests.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter, to make these silly folks so full of gratitude.

SCROOGE
Small! Fezziwig had the power to render us happy or unhappy, to make our service a pleasure or a toil. The happiness he gave was quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

The Spirit fixes him with a gentle look.

SCROOGE
I should like to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

INT. BELLE'S PARLOUR - EVENING

YOUNG SCROOGE, older and leaner, retains his dark hair and black coat, but avarice now hardens his brown eyes. BELLE, a fair young woman in a simple mourning dress, sits opposite him beside the fire.

(SOUND: a clock ticks between their measured breaths)

BELLE
Another idol has displaced me.

YOUNG SCROOGE
What idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one. I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you.

YOUNG SCROOGE
Have I ever sought release?

BELLE
In words? No. Never. You are changed. I release you, with a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

The spectral light shifts. Years pass within the room. BELLE, now a warm-faced matron, laughs beside her husband and children while a vision in the frosted window shows Scrooge alone at his desk.

(MUSIC: the dance melody returns slowly on mournful strings)

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more! Why do you delight to torture me?

He seizes the Spirit's great extinguisher-cap and forces it over the blazing head.

(SOUND: light roars from beneath the cap like a rushing flame)

The room disappears in white.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

Scrooge, again the gaunt old man with white hair and a faded dressing gown, wakes to find his chamber transformed into a grove of holly, ivy and mistletoe. A mountain of Christmas food surrounds a roaring hearth.

(SOUND: fire thunders up the chimney, chestnuts crack and punch bubbles)

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT, a joyful giant with long brown curls, a holly wreath and a loose green robe trimmed in white fur, raises a glowing torch.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in, and know me better, man! I am the Ghost of Christmas Present.

SCROOGE
Spirit, conduct me where you will. I learnt a lesson which is working now. If you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

Scrooge obeys. The chamber vanishes in warm golden light.

(MUSIC: distant Christmas voices rise beneath the wind)

INT. CRATCHIT COTTAGE - CHRISTMAS DAY

A humble four-roomed home glows around one precious fire. MRS. CRATCHIT, a worn, kind-faced woman in a twice-turned dress brightened by ribbons, sets a small goose before her eager children.

(SOUND: plates clatter, potatoes bubble and a little crutch taps toward the hearth)

Bob Cratchit enters in his threadbare clothes and long white comforter, carrying TINY TIM. Tim is a small, pale boy with brown curls, a wooden crutch and iron braces supporting his thin legs.

BOB CRATCHIT
He hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember who made lame beggars walk and blind men see.

Bob seats Tim close beside him. The family devours the goose, then cheers as Mrs. Cratchit brings in a small pudding blazing with brandy.

(SOUND: the family cheers as the flame sputters and the pudding lands on the table)

Bob raises a handleless cup.

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us, every one!

Scrooge watches Tim's withered hand resting inside Bob's.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! Say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
What then? If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge bows beneath his own words.

(MUSIC: a single sorrowful violin beneath the family's fading laughter)

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - CHRISTMAS DAY

Fred, still ruddy and handsome in his brown coat, laughs beside FRED'S WIFE, a young woman with dark curls and a burgundy dress. Friends gather around their firelit table.

(SOUND: hearty laughter, glasses chiming and a harp playing softly)

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

FRED'S WIFE
More shame for him, Fred!

FRED
I am sorry for him. Who suffers by his ill whims? Himself, always. I mean to give him the same chance every year, whether he likes it or not.

Invisible beside the Spirit, Scrooge begins laughing with them.

FRED
A Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year to the old man, whatever he is! Uncle Scrooge!

Fred drinks. Scrooge raises an empty hand in answer.

EXT. LONDON CHURCH SQUARE - MIDNIGHT

Snow whirls across a deserted square. The Ghost of Christmas Present now stoops with age, its brown curls turned grey beneath the holly wreath.

(SOUND: the clock mechanism grinds toward midnight, wind hissing over the stones)

Two starved children crawl from beneath the green robe. IGNORANCE, a wolfish boy in rags, and WANT, a gaunt girl with hollow eyes, clutch the Spirit's feet.

(MUSIC: low, dissonant strings gather beneath the wind)

SCROOGE
Spirit, are they yours?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
They are Man's. This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

(SOUND: twelve iron bell strokes shake the square)

The Ghost and children vanish. The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME glides from the mist, tall and faceless beneath a deep black shroud, with only one pale hand visible.

SCROOGE
Ghost of the Future! I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. Lead on.

The silent hand points into darkness.

INT. PLUNDERED BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

A bare bed stands in a cold, stripped room. Beneath a ragged sheet lies a solitary corpse; even the bed-curtains have been torn down.

(SOUND: rats gnaw beneath the hearthstone and a cat scratches at the locked door)

The Ghost of Christmas Yet to Come points toward the covered face. Scrooge reaches for the sheet, but his hand freezes above it.

SCROOGE
This is a fearful place. In leaving it, I shall not leave its lesson, trust me.

The corpse's rigid hand hangs over the mattress. No mourner comes.

(MUSIC: a low funeral pulse, distant and unresolved)

SCROOGE
Let me see some tenderness connected with a death, or this dark chamber will be forever present to me.

The black robe spreads across the room.

INT. CRATCHIT COTTAGE - EVENING

The same cottage is painfully quiet. Mrs. Cratchit and her older children sew beside the fire. Tiny Tim's crutch rests beside his empty stool.

(SOUND: a needle draws through cloth, the clock ticks and no crutch answers)

Bob enters slowly. His brown hair is disordered, and his white comforter hangs over the same threadbare clothes.

BOB CRATCHIT
I wish you could have gone with me. It would have done you good to see how green a place it is. I promised him that I would walk there on a Sunday.

Bob touches the empty stool.

BOB CRATCHIT
My little, little child!

(SOUND: Bob's restrained sob breaks the silence)

Mrs. Cratchit and the children gather around him. Scrooge turns desperately to the hooded Ghost.

SCROOGE
Tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead.

The Ghost points through the wall toward the night.

(MUSIC: the funeral pulse becomes a slow heartbeat)

EXT. LONDON CHURCHYARD - NIGHT

Crooked stones crowd a neglected churchyard choked with weeds. The Ghost stands beside one grave and points down.

(SOUND: wind scrapes dead branches across stone, a distant bell tolls)

SCROOGE
Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

The hand remains fixed. Scrooge creeps closer and wipes snow from the stone.

(SOUND: a pounding heartbeat rises as his fingers scrape the frozen surface)

The inscription reads: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed?

The finger points from the grave to Scrooge.

SCROOGE
No, Spirit! Hear me! I am not the man I was. Why show me this, if I am past all hope?

The spectral hand trembles.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me!

Scrooge clutches the shroud.

SCROOGE
Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

(MUSIC: the funeral pulse surges into a bright, ascending chord)

The black folds engulf him, collapse and dwindle into a carved bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Golden sunlight fills the chamber. Scrooge, still gaunt with wispy white hair and wiry whiskers, embraces his intact bed-curtains, his lined face transformed by joy.

(SOUND: church bells peal wildly across clear morning air)

SCROOGE
They are not torn down, rings and all! I am here! The shadows of the things that would have been may be dispelled!

He laughs, tangles himself in his stockings and runs to the window.

SCROOGE
I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a schoolboy! A merry Christmas to everybody!

Below stands TOM, a sharp-eyed street boy in a patched blue coat and Sunday cap.

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

TOM
Why, Christmas Day!

SCROOGE
I haven't missed it! Do you know the Poulterer's in the next street? Go and buy the great prize Turkey. Come back in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

Tom shoots away. Soon an enormous turkey rolls through the snow on a handcart, bearing Scrooge's card addressed to Bob Cratchit in Camden Town.

(SOUND: cart wheels rattle while Scrooge's laughter rings from the window)

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Scrooge, dressed in his best black coat and polished hat, walks among bright shop windows and smiling crowds. He spots Mr. Dobson, recognisable by his grey whiskers and brown coat.

(SOUND: passing greetings, carriage wheels and cheerful church bells)

SCROOGE
My dear sir, allow me to ask your pardon.

Scrooge whispers an amount into Mr. Dobson's ear.

MR. DOBSON
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it. Will you do me that favour?

MR. DOBSON
My dear sir, I don't know what to say.

SCROOGE
Don't say anything. Come and see me. Bless you!

They clasp hands.

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - CHRISTMAS DAY

Fred and his dark-curled wife arrange the laden table. Scrooge's tentative face appears around the door.

(SOUND: silverware stops clinking and the room falls expectantly still)

FRED
Why, bless my soul! Who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred rushes forward and embraces him.

(SOUND: chairs scrape back and the whole company erupts in welcome)

(MUSIC: the harp takes up a bright Christmas air)

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY'S COUNTING HOUSE - MORNING

The next morning, Scrooge sits behind his ledger with his office door wide open. Bob Cratchit, still slight and brown-haired in his threadbare clothes and white comforter, slips late into his cold little cell.

(SOUND: the clock strikes eighteen minutes past nine, followed by Bob's frantic quill)

SCROOGE
What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. It's only once a year. I was making rather merry yesterday.

SCROOGE
I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer.

Bob shrinks toward the ruler. Scrooge leaps from his stool and claps him on the back.

SCROOGE
And therefore I am about to raise your salary! A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas than I have given you for many a year!

Bob stares, speechless.

SCROOGE
I'll assist your struggling family. Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

(SOUND: the coal-box opens and a shovel plunges joyfully into it)

INT. CRATCHIT COTTAGE - CHRISTMAS DAY

One year later, the cottage glows with a generous fire. Scrooge, gaunt and white-haired but rosy with happiness, steadies Tiny Tim, still small and brown-curled with his crutch and iron braces, as the boy crosses the hearth.

(SOUND: Tim's crutch taps firmly amid the family's warm laughter)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge was better than his word. To Tiny Tim, who did not die, he was a second father. It was always said of him that he knew how to keep Christmas well.

Scrooge lifts Tim into his chair beside the fire.

(MUSIC: full strings and Christmas bells rise together)

TINY TIM
God bless us, every one!

FADE OUT.

THE END
